00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:39.270
capital spending cycle rather than headline AI hype. Before we check how yesterday's flash trade

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:43.970
performed here's a quick reminder on passive versus active realities.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:53.410
Imagine you bought an S&amp;P 500 ETF last week when the index dipped 0.8% on geopolitical headlines.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:06:00.590
Your fill came at the exact closing NAV because the ETF's creation redemption mechanism keeps

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:07.110
tracking tight. The mechanism works because authorized participants arbitrage any premium or

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:15.370
discount in real time keeping expense ratios under 0.05% while delivering the index return minus

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:21.730
that tiny fee. What most retail investors do not realize is that the same low cost structure

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:28.210
also prevents tax loss harvesting opportunities that active managers can exploit inside the same

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:36.590
account type. Professionals always check the SPV a scorecard before deciding. Over 85% of active

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:44.330
large cap funds have underperformed the S&amp;P 500 over 15 years. Yet the same data shows active

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:49.850
small cap and emerging market managers beat their benchmarks roughly 40% of the time.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:55.350
The biggest mistake with the passive versus active debate is treating it as an all or nothing

00:06:55.350 --> 00:07:03.750
choice. Instead always run a core satellite test. Keep 70% in low cost index ETFs for market beta.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:11.830
Then allocate the remaining 30% to active sleeves only where the SPI VA outperformance rate exceeds 50%

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:18.550
and your own due diligence confirms repeatable edge. Speaking of evidence, let's review yesterday's

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:25.550
flash trade result. Yesterday's flash trade was MU on an AI momentum entry using an earnings

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:36.390
revision catalyst with volume confirmation. We entered at $1,233 at market open and exited at $1,213

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:46.970
and 56 cents at market close. The result was a loss of 1.58% or $15.77 on a $1,000 position.

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:58.230
The trade lost 1.58% while the NASDAQ fell 0.2% over the same session for negative 1.38% alpha.

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:04.030
The earnings revision catalyst reversed intraday once broader cost concerns resurfaced.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:08.750
This confirms that single-day momentum entries require tighter risk controls.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:15.050
The new rule is to require closing price confirmation before declaring any catalyst trade complete.

